% ============================================================================
% ocean-zone-scientific-name — the midnight zone → its alternate scientific
% name, decoded from a span already sitting unused inside the SAME WHOI
% source `ocean-zones.adj` and `ocean-zone-depth.adj` already cite
% (adj-facts-stdlib, OCEANOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `ocean-zones.adj` (`ocean_zone(zone,
% order)`) and `ocean-zone-depth.adj` (`ocean_zone_depth(zone,
% max_depth_meters)`): a new `ocean_zone_scientific_name(zone,
% scientific_name)` table names the alternate scientific name the SAME
% already-cited WHOI "Ocean Zones" page states for the midnight zone,
% decoded from a clause already sitting unused inside both siblings' own
% header truth tables -- no new WebFetch. Neither parent table's schema has
% a column for an alternate NAME (one tables sequence position, the other
% tables depth); the already-quoted midnight-zone sentence also carries a
% second, scientific name for that zone that never made it into a row:
%
%   midnight_zone → bathypelagic
%     "The midnight zone, or bathypelagic, extends to about 4,000 meters
%      (about 13,100 feet)"
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? ocean_zone_scientific_name(midnight_zone, $ScientificName)  % bathypelagic
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like both
% siblings' own): the single `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `ocean_zone_scientific_name(zone, scientific_name)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the
% engine returns the name WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any zone that is not the one tabled here (it
% never invents a name). The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind a scientific
% name and recall which zone it names:
%
%     ? ocean_zone_scientific_name($Zone, bathypelagic)   % midnight_zone
%
% This is honestly a ONE-ROW table, not a forced three-row one: the
% sunlight-zone and twilight-zone spans already quoted inside both sibling
% files' own headers do NOT include an alternate scientific name for
% either zone (only the midnight-zone span does) -- so this table
% correctly abstains on sunlight_zone and twilight_zone rather than
% inventing "epipelagic"/"mesopelagic" names the cited source never
% states.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME midnight-zone span
% already quoted inside `ocean-zone-depth.adj`'s own header/provenance
% section, from the SAME Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution (WHOI)
% "Ocean Zones" page both siblings already cite -- no new WebFetch, only a
% different schema decoding a part of that already-verified quote. `trust
% consensus` -- the same tier both siblings already carry (WHOI is a
% reputable, long-running oceanographic research institution but not a
% .gov domain).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table ocean_zone_scientific_name {
    columns zone, scientific_name

    row (midnight_zone, bathypelagic)

    source "The midnight zone, or bathypelagic, extends to about 4,000 meters (about 13,100 feet), which reaches the ocean floor in many places is in perpetual darkness."
    locator "https://www.whoi.edu/know-your-ocean/ocean-topics/how-the-ocean-works/ocean-zones/"
    trust consensus
}
